artifact.self_contained_problem:
Consider a chemical reaction network (CRN) modeled as a finite set of chemical species $\{X_i\}_{i=1}^N$ together with a finite set of elementary reactions $\{\rho\}$, each written as $\sum_i \nu^-_{i\rho} X_i \rightleftharpoons \sum_i \nu^+_{i\rho} X_i$ with stoichiometric matrix $S_{i\rho} = \nu^+_{i\rho} - \nu^-_{i\rho}$. In the standard setting, the network evolves in a homogeneous, isothermal, dilute (ideal) solution at fixed temperature $T$, and a consistent nonequilibrium thermodynamic framework assigns to each reaction a chemical affinity, an entropy production rate $\dot\Sigma \ge 0$, and a free-energy balance derived from local detailed balance with rate constants satisfying $k^+_\rho/k^-_\rho = \exp\!\big(-\sum_i S_{i\rho}\mu^\circ_i/(k_B T)\big)$, where $\mu^\circ_i$ are standard chemical potentials.

Develop a mathematically consistent extension of this nonequilibrium thermodynamic framework for CRNs that simultaneously incorporates:
(a) nonisothermal effects, in which the temperature $T(\mathbf r,t)$ is a dynamical field coupled to chemical kinetics through reaction enthalpies and a heat equation;
(b) non-ideal solutions, in which the chemical potentials take the form $\mu_i = \mu^\circ_i + k_B T \ln a_i(\{c_j\},T)$ with activities $a_i$ derived from a non-ideal free-energy density (e.g.\ Flory–Huggins or Debye–Hückel), and reaction rates and local detailed balance are modified accordingly;
(c) photochemistry, in which a subset of reactions is driven by absorption/emission of photons from a radiation field with prescribed spectral intensity, with the corresponding entropy and free-energy exchange properly accounted for;
(d) electrochemistry, in which charged species couple to an electrostatic potential $\phi(\mathbf r,t)$ determined self-consistently (e.g.\ via Poisson–Nernst–Planck equations), and reaction affinities include the electrochemical potential $\tilde\mu_i = \mu_i + z_i e\phi$.

The required output is: a unified set of dynamical equations for concentrations, temperature, and electrostatic/radiation fields; a definition of total entropy production rate that is non-negative and decomposes additively into chemical, thermal, photochemical, and electrochemical contributions; a corresponding free-energy/Lyapunov functional whose dissipation equals the entropy production at the appropriate temperature; and proofs that, in suitable closed/detailed-balanced limits, the framework reduces to the standard isothermal ideal-solution CRN thermodynamics. Establish, in particular, the analogue of the second law and of the fluctuation theorems in this combined setting.

